---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const b = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp');
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Postscript or Mailchimp for cart recovery?',
    answer: 'For a team already using Mailchimp email, evaluate whether adding an SMS workflow solves a distinct recovery problem. Compare eligibility, offer ownership, purchase cancellation and operating cost across both senders. This comparison establishes no universal winner.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How should we compare coupon cost?',
    answer: 'Use hypothetical $334 and $486 carts to check incentive handling, not to infer product performance. Keep product costs fixed when applying a discount and verify stacking and refund treatment.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Channel escalation?',
    answer: 'For a Mailchimp email recipient, check separate SMS marketing permission before any Postscript branch, assign a queue owner per channel, and recheck purchase and suppression state at dispatch.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Attribution caution?',
    answer: 'Propose a randomized holdout sized for adequate power. Compare contribution after discounts and matched all-in costs; platform attribution alone cannot establish incremental recovery.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Migration tip?',
    answer: 'Map email and SMS queue ownership first. Validate permission, purchase exits and replay deduplication with test events; pause the old sender before enabling its replacement and reconcile queued messages at cutover.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Margin math?',
    answer: '$334 full-price $110.22 vs 10% code $76.82 (Δ $33.4).',
  },
  {
    question: 'Who should choose each?',
    answer: 'Evaluate Postscript for the SMS requirements and Mailchimp for the existing email workflow. Verify current integration and plan support, then choose based on permission handling, queue ownership, purchase exits and matched operating costs; these fixtures establish no winner.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Workflow test?',
    answer: 'Test an email-only subscriber, a separately opted-in SMS recipient, a purchase between queued messages and a replayed cart event. Confirm which sender owns each message and that both queues cancel ineligible sends.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">For a Shopify team evaluating SMS alongside an existing Mailchimp email program, the decision is who owns each recovery message and how both systems receive purchase and suppression updates. The $334 and $486 carts below are hypothetical planning fixtures, not merchant results.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Evaluate SMS', tool: a.name, reason: 'Define the additional eligible audience and who owns the SMS queue.' }, { label: 'Retain email', tool: b.name, reason: 'Keep the working email baseline while measuring the cost and value of adding SMS.' }]} />
      </div>
      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>{a.name}</th><th>{b.name}</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Decision in this guide</td><td>Add a separately eligible SMS recovery workflow</td><td>Retain the existing email workflow as the baseline</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Offer ownership</td><td>Consume the agreed recovery offer without issuing an accidental second coupon</td><td>Record the offer already promised by email before any SMS handoff</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Purchase cancellation</td><td>Recheck eligibility before the proposed SMS dispatch</td><td>Cancel queued email for the completed cart and pass purchase state onward</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cost comparison</td><td>Include message charges, integration work and exception handling</td><td>Include retained platform costs and the migration work avoided</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Evaluate the email and SMS handoff</h2>
        <p>Start with a Mailchimp email subscriber who has no documented SMS marketing permission: a cart event must not silently enroll that person in an SMS recovery sequence. Then use a test profile with separate permission for each channel. Assign one queue owner per channel and a shared cart identifier; replay the same event and confirm it does not schedule another message. Verify current plan and integration support for these requirements before selecting either product.</p>
        <p>For the same test cart, simulate a purchase after email is queued but before SMS is dispatched. Check both queues against current purchase and suppression state at dispatch, including when a webhook arrives late. Record which system cancels which message and who handles a failed cancellation. Compare the cost of keeping the existing email setup and adding SMS with the cost of replacing it, using the same audience, message volume, implementation effort and support requirements.</p>
        <p>The cases below are hypothetical planning situations for an existing email program and a proposed SMS handoff. The decision cases propose integration checks, not observed tests or vendor capabilities. Choose scheduling and contact limits from the merchant’s purpose, permissions and operating requirements; no universal delay or cap is implied. Contribution here means revenue less the assumed product cost and discount; deduct delivery, platform, payment, fulfillment and other matched variable costs separately.</p>
        <p><strong>Retain email without adding SMS.</strong> An email-only Mailchimp subscriber abandons a cart. Keep the recovery decision in the email workflow; a phone number on the order is not evidence of SMS marketing permission. Adding Postscript creates no eligible SMS audience for this profile.</p>
        <p><strong>Separate channel permission.</strong> A shopper has documented SMS marketing permission but no email marketing permission for this purpose. Route only the eligible SMS message to its owner. Do not copy email subscription state into the SMS field, or infer email permission from the SMS record.</p>
        <p><strong>Both channels eligible.</strong> For a dual-channel test profile, decide whether SMS replaces a queued email or supplements it before enabling either branch. Record the cart and message IDs in a shared ledger so the two senders do not independently spend the same incentive budget.</p>
        <p><strong>Permission withdrawn while queued.</strong> Withdraw SMS marketing permission after Postscript queues a message while leaving email permission unchanged. Cancel the SMS branch and re-evaluate the email independently; a channel-specific withdrawal must not be overwritten by a later cart sync.</p>
        <p><strong>Purpose mismatch.</strong> A phone number supplied for delivery updates does not by itself establish permission for a marketing cart reminder. Keep the delivery notification purpose separate from the proposed Postscript recovery purpose in the eligibility record.</p>
        <p><strong>Coupon ownership.</strong> If Mailchimp email already contains a recovery code, assign one system to issue and retire that code. The SMS handoff should reference the intended offer rather than automatically minting a second code with a different expiry.</p>
        <p><strong>Welcome offer collision.</strong> A shopper receives a welcome offer and then enters recovery. Compare the actual permitted combined discount with the single-code fixture below. If Shopify allows stacking, calculate that combined cost before deciding which sender can make an offer.</p>
        <p><strong>Expired code in a queued message.</strong> Expire the email code before the proposed SMS dispatch. Decide whether to cancel the SMS or generate an explicitly approved replacement; forwarding a stale email offer transfers a broken promise to the second channel.</p>
        <p><strong>Cart value changes.</strong> Remove an item after the email is queued. Recompute the offer against the current cart before handing it to SMS; the original AOV calculation is a planning example, not authority to discount the smaller cart.</p>
        <p><strong>Unavailable variant.</strong> Make the selected variant unavailable between the two queues. Stop the stale recovery link and choose whether an eligible alternative-product message is appropriate. Do not treat an SMS reminder as a remedy for unavailable inventory.</p>
        <p><strong>Late purchase event.</strong> Create an order before dispatch, then delay its event delivery. Inspect the current order state used by both senders and record any stale send. A fixed grace period cannot establish that a purchaser is still eligible.</p>
        <p><strong>Purchase during handoff.</strong> Purchase after the email sends and before SMS dispatch. The SMS owner must receive the cancellation decision; email delivery alone must not be interpreted as proof that recovery remains open.</p>
        <p><strong>Replayed purchase event.</strong> Deliver the same purchase event twice. Both attempts should resolve to the same cancellation outcome without issuing another coupon or reopening the cart. Store the event identity separately from the shopper identity.</p>
        <p><strong>Replayed cart event.</strong> Deliver the identical abandonment event twice to the proposed integration. Compare scheduled message IDs, not only the count visible in one dashboard; a second sender can hide the duplicate in a different queue.</p>
        <p><strong>Legitimate cart update.</strong> Change a quantity and submit a new event for the same cart. Accept the update without treating it as another abandonment campaign. Deduplication by cart ID alone can discard a valid price or stock change.</p>
        <p><strong>Two devices, one shopper.</strong> Join a guest cart with an identified shopper only when the identity match is supported. Inspect whether the join leaves two live recovery queues; a shared email address is not a substitute for tracing both cart records.</p>
        <p><strong>Contact merge conflict.</strong> Merge records with conflicting SMS permission evidence. Preserve the provenance and unresolved state instead of selecting the more permissive flag. Keep the eligible email queue separate while the SMS conflict is resolved.</p>
        <p><strong>Cutover with queued email.</strong> If replacing an email workflow, export the queued message identifiers and reconcile cancellation before enabling its replacement. If retaining Mailchimp, leave its email ownership explicit and enable only the proposed SMS responsibility.</p>
        <p><strong>Retry after sender timeout.</strong> Simulate a dispatch request whose response is lost. Reconcile the provider message identifier before retrying; a timeout does not prove non-delivery. Include this exception work in the cost of operating two senders.</p>
        <p><strong>Reporting overlap.</strong> When both dashboards attribute one order, reconcile the Shopify order ID before summing revenue. Compare randomized eligible groups for incremental contribution; a second attributed conversion is not a second order.</p>
        <p><strong>Operator cost.</strong> Track staff time spent resolving permission conflicts, stale codes and failed cancellations in a proposed pilot. Multiply time by the merchant’s own loaded rate, then add it to matched platform and delivery costs for retain-email-plus-SMS versus replacement.</p>
        <p><strong>Decision to defer SMS.</strong> If the team cannot establish SMS eligibility or reconcile purchase cancellations across queues, retain the working email path while repairing that gap. The incentive arithmetic below cannot justify launching an unowned channel.</p>
        <h2>Cart recovery workflow test</h2>
        <p>Keep the existing Mailchimp email sequence as the baseline. For a hypothetical $334 cart, queue email, then record a purchase before the proposed Postscript SMS dispatch. A purchase exit passes only if neither sender sends a recovery message for that completed cart. Repeat with a delayed purchase event: if cancellation cannot be confirmed, keep the second channel paused rather than treating webhook receipt as proof that its queue was cleared. The $486 cart is a separate incentive fixture; a higher cart value alone does not justify a larger discount.</p>
        <h2>Channel escalation</h2>
        <p>Adding SMS gives the team another way to contact a separately eligible shopper, but also adds a second queue, permission record and operating cost. Keep email responsible for the detailed cart explanation and use a proposed SMS branch only for a distinct, useful message. Agree a combined contact budget before enabling it; separate per-tool caps cannot prevent two systems from contacting the same shopper together.</p>
        <h2>Attribution</h2>
        <p>Propose an email-only baseline against email plus SMS for shoppers eligible for both channels. Randomize at shopper level so repeated carts remain in the same group; size the experiment for adequate power and compare contribution after discounts and matched all-in costs. Platform-attributed revenue does not establish causal incrementality; use the randomized holdout to estimate lift.</p>
        <h2>Retain email or replace the workflow?</h2>
        <p>Retain the current email program when its segments, templates and reporting still meet the team’s requirements and the missing job is a separately permitted SMS branch. Replacing email at the same time makes it harder to distinguish an SMS effect from changes in email content, audience or timing. Consider replacement only when a concrete email requirement is unmet and the replacement has been demonstrated against that requirement; this comparison does not establish that either product can replace the other.</p>
        <p>For a replay test, submit the same cart event twice with the same event identifier, then submit an updated cart with a new event identifier. The first replay should not create another message; the legitimate update should not be discarded merely because the shopper already has a cart record. Keep a ledger of cart ID, event ID, channel, scheduled message ID and cancellation outcome across the two senders. If the team cannot reconcile those records, adding a second sender creates an operational gap even if each individual message looks correct.</p>
        <p>Compare two budgets using the same eligible shoppers and monthly message volume: current email plus proposed SMS, and the proposed replacement workflow. Include platform charges, message and carrier charges where applicable, integration maintenance, template work and the staff time needed to reconcile exceptions. Credit only incremental contribution from the experiment, not the sum of both platforms’ attributed revenue. Retaining email is attractive when it avoids migration work; that advantage can disappear if manual queue reconciliation consumes the saving.</p>
        <h2>Who should choose {a.name}</h2>
        <p>Evaluate adding an SMS owner when the existing email workflow is worth retaining and the team can demonstrate separate SMS marketing eligibility, current purchase cancellation and offer ownership across both queues. This is a selection requirement, not a verified capability claim.</p>
        <h2>Who should choose {b.name}</h2>
        <p>Evaluate retaining the existing email workflow when its eligible audience, templates and purchase exits meet the merchant’s needs. Compare the incremental work of an SMS handoff with the migration and maintenance cost of replacement; neither option wins from the fixture calculations alone.</p>
      </article>
      <div class="prose-shopify mt-10">
      <h2>Coupon cost before channel cost</h2>
      <p>These are hypothetical carts, not merchant outcomes. At $334 AOV and a 33% product margin, the assumed product cost is $223.78. Full-price contribution is $110.22; a 10% code reduces revenue by $33.40 and contribution to $76.82. On a $486 cart with the same margin and discount, product cost is $325.62, full-price contribution $160.38 and discounted contribution $111.78: a $48.60 reduction. Product cost stays fixed when applying the code.</p>
      <p>If email already offered the code, adding SMS does not make that discount free or create a second recovered order. Attribute the order once, then deduct the incentive and matched delivery, payment, fulfillment, platform and operator costs. Compare full-price and discounted groups only under a design that can distinguish customers who would have purchased anyway. These examples price the offer; they do not predict the conversion effect.</p>
      <h2>Product scope and evidence</h2>
      <p><a href="https://postscript.io/features">Postscript’s feature page</a> describes an SMS program and connections with email providers. <a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp’s Shopify connection guide</a> documents linking a store to a Mailchimp audience. These sources support evaluating connected workflows; they do not establish that a specific two-sender cancellation or coupon handoff works in the merchant’s setup. The cases above are proposed acceptance checks.</p>
      <p>This guide considers adding SMS alongside existing email; it does not claim either product is restricted to that channel or that either can replace every function of the other. Obtain current plan, country and channel eligibility and a matched quote for the intended scope. No static rating, entry price or vendor revenue claim determines the choice here.</p>
    </div>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="{a.name} vs {b.name} FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
